Z||000000||FREHLEY1||07-10-2001||06:59 AM||||I HAVE A 88 FORMULA 5 spd WITH A "SICK" 2.8 AND WAS WONDERING HOW HARD IT IS TO SWITCH IT OVER TO A 3800. THANKS!!!<P>HI EVERYONE IT'S ME AGAIN...I WAS JUST INFORMED THAT A 3.4 IS A MUCH EASIER SWAP. LET ME KNOW THE PRO'S AND CON'S PLEASE. <p>[This message has been edited by FREHLEY1 (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||07-10-2001||10:03 AM||||Not trying to oversimplify, but:<BR>3.4 is cheaper, but less power.<BR>3800 is more expensive, but more power.<P>The 3.4 is almost a straight drop in, but the 3800 requires more fabrication/custom work.  Contact West Coast Fiero about info on the 3800 (or 3.4?) swap.  Ed Park's Fiero Factory can also help with the 3.4 swap, and has detailed info on what's necessary.<P>Also, if originality matters, the 3.4 can be dressed to look identical to the original 2.8.  That illusion gets blown out of the water with the 3800.  And a well built 3.4 can just about match the factory output of a normall aspirated 3800 (205HP).||||reg||6
Z||000002||West Coast Fiero||07-10-2001||11:46 AM||||Formula88 is right - how fast you want to go is in direct correlation as to how deep your pockets are unfortunately.<P> To give a rough as sandpaper estimate, the 3.4 single cam can run about $3500 - $4000, where as the 3800 Series 1 and 2 can run anywhere from $6000 to $7000 - but if you choose to go this route the difference between the Series 1 and 2 is about 10hp and $300, with the Series 2 being the more expensive and having 10 horse over the Series 1. Any questions at all just give us a call.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||07-10-2001||01:11 PM||||I hope your estimates include a shop doing the install. Otherwise there a bit high.<P>JM<P>||||reg||1
